// Heart: what the regulars say as they get to know you, and the radio between calls.
// HEART[id] = [stranger, knows your name, friend, best friend]. Each line unlocks once with the friendship and then
// comes back now and then. The last line is the thing they don't tell people. Funny first, honest later, like people.

export const HEART = {
  delgado: ['Mustard on the left, ketchup on the right, opinions are free. You want a dog, or you just looking?',
    'Cadet! Same as last time? Don\'t answer, I already started it. You hesitate, you eat what I give you.',
    'My middle daughter, she\'s doing her residency now. Pediatrics. Calls every Sunday to ask if I\'m sitting down enough. I\'m sitting right now. Look. Sitting.',
    'You know why I never miss a day? My first week here, a cop bought two dogs and gave one to a man asleep on that bench. Didn\'t wake him. Just left it there. I thought, okay. This is a place where people do that. I want to be here in case it happens again.'],
  pruitt: ['Don\'t step on Gerald. The gray one. He\'s slow and he holds a grudge.',
    'You walk it like you mean it now. Heel, toe, eyes up. Took me eight years to stop looking at my shoes.',
    'My wife used to make me empty my pockets on the porch before I came in. Said she didn\'t want the day coming inside. Six years she\'s been gone. I still do it. Habit is a kind of company.',
    'March of \'88 I had a call I don\'t talk about. A kid. I did everything right and it didn\'t matter. Next morning I came out here and fed these birds because I didn\'t know what else to do with my hands. Some things you don\'t get over, cadet. You build a routine around them.'],
  dot: ['Sit anywhere but booth four. Menu\'s on the wall. The wall doesn\'t lie. The specials board does.',
    'Cream and two sugars. Told you. Never wrong about cops and coffee. Husbands, sometimes.',
    'Three a.m. is the honest hour, hon. Everybody in here at three is working, grieving, or in love. Sometimes all three. I just keep the cups full.',
    'Henry tipped in quarters. Said bills were for rent and quarters were for the jukebox. When I heard it on the radio I was holding his cup. Didn\'t put it down for an hour. I still pour it. I know it\'s silly. Don\'t tell me it\'s silly.'],
  marcus: ['Elevator in the Meridian lies about floor six. Take the stairs. You\'re welcome. Gotta go.',
    'You again! You\'re everywhere. You\'re me with a car. And a pension. And knees.',
    'People think messengers are reckless. Eleven years, never hit anybody. I\'m careful. I just look fast because everybody else is standing still.',
    'Real talk? I ride because when I stop, I think. Some nights I do the whole downtown loop after work just to get tired enough to sleep. You probably know what I mean. You\'ve got the face.'],
  okafor: ['If you\'re here for chapter five, it\'s checked out. If you\'re here for the restroom, it\'s past the atlases.',
    'I set a book aside for you. No, you didn\'t ask. Librarians guess. We\'re usually right.',
    'My mother taught forty children with nine books. Made them read out loud in turns so the books would last. I think of her every time someone tells me the Wi-Fi is slow.',
    'When we came here I was eleven and I didn\'t talk like the other kids. The librarian at the old branch let me stay until closing every day and never asked why. I didn\'t have the words to thank her. So I became her. This building is my thank-you note.'],
  kevin: ['If it\'s the terminal, restart it. If it\'s the radio, restart it. If it\'s your life, I can\'t help you. I\'m Tier 1.',
    'Hey! You\'re the one who actually restarts things. You\'re my favorite. It\'s a short list. You and a printer.',
    'Nobody notices IT when it works. That\'s the job. If I\'m good, I\'m invisible. I\'m extremely good. Some days I wonder if I exist.',
    'The old server downstairs still has the first dispatch log. February 1993. First entry is Velvet saying "Good morning, Port Meridian." Some nights when the building\'s empty I pull it up and read it. I don\'t know why. It\'s nice, knowing how things started.'],
  rosa: ['You need something or you\'re just looking? Everybody just looks. Nobody buys the plantains.',
    'Mijo, take a water. No, take it. You look like a raisin. Pay me next time. Or never. I have a notebook.',
    'The notebook? People who couldn\'t pay in 2008. I don\'t erase them. Not to collect. So I remember who stayed in this neighborhood when it was hard. Every name in there still comes in.',
    'I almost closed in 2009. Sign was written. Then Mrs. Paredes came in with an envelope. Nineteen dollars, from the whole block. "For the light bill." Nineteen dollars. I cried in the walk-in so nobody would see. Good place to cry, a freezer. Nothing runs.'],
  earl: ['If it\'s making a noise, describe the noise. Don\'t do the noise. Everybody does the noise.',
    'Your unit\'s brakes squeak. I can hear it from here. Bring it by. Don\'t tell the motor pool.',
    'See the handprint in the slab? My dad. Poured it in \'79, put his hand in before it set. Every morning I stand my boots next to it. Size thirteen. Both of us.',
    'Dad fixed patrol cars free in the \'83 storm. People think he did it for the cops. My mom was a nurse at County, and cops were the only ones who could drive her in. He never said that out loud. I figured somebody should know it was for love.'],
  reyes: ['Here to train or to watch? Watching costs nothing. Training costs everything. Water\'s free either way.',
    'I bet your guard drops when you\'re tired. Everybody\'s does. Mine still does, and I\'m fifty-one.',
    'Kids come in here mad at the whole world. I don\'t tell them not to be mad. I tell them where to put it. Bag\'s over there. The bag doesn\'t press charges.',
    'I lost one. Tomás. Sixteen, best jab I ever saw. He stopped coming in one March and I didn\'t go looking. Figured he\'d be back. Now when anybody misses a week, I knock on their door. Every time. Even when it\'s embarrassing. Especially then.'],
  kim: ['Machines eight and eleven eat quarters. There\'s a sign. Nobody reads the sign. Then they yell at me.',
    'You fold like a soldier. Good. People who fold well are honest. I made that up. It\'s usually true.',
    'Big dryer at the end, I named her Min-ji. My wife\'s name. Twelve years, never broken once. My son says that\'s a coincidence. My son also thinks cereal is dinner.',
    'I sold her piano to buy this place. She played every night. Now when I close, I sit next to Min-ji while the last load finishes. The tumbling sounds a little like... no. It doesn\'t. I sit there anyway.'],
  ruth: ['Don\'t you rush past my porch. I\'ve been watching this street since before your mama was born.',
    'You went ten-eight without telling dispatch you were back. I heard. I have a scanner and nothing but time.',
    'Twenty-eight years on the sheriff\'s switchboard, nights. I talked deputies home through things they\'d never tell their wives. The good ones still call on my birthday.',
    'Worst night, 1974, a deputy keyed up and didn\'t say a thing. Just breathing. I kept talking. Weather, ballgames, my tomatoes. Forty minutes. Then, "Thank you, Ruthie." I never told a soul. I\'m telling you because someday you\'ll be on one end of that or the other.'],
  dez: ['I\'m not doing anything. I\'m sitting. On my board. Which is legal. I checked.',
    'Oh, it\'s you. Okay. You\'re cool. You\'re like ninety percent cool. The hat costs you ten.',
    'The cop who caught me tagging at twelve gave me this board instead of a ticket. Said if I wanted to leave marks on the city, use wheels. Corny, right? Still worked.',
    'My brother didn\'t get a board. Different cop, same wall, two years earlier. He got a record. He\'s thirty and still can\'t get hired anywhere decent. Same kid, same wall, different day. Be the board guy. Please.'],
  hal: ['Slips are full, the ramp closes at dark, life jackets aren\'t optional. That\'s the whole speech.',
    'Wind\'s coming around southeast. Feel that? No, you don\'t. You\'re from inland. You\'ll learn.',
    'Forty years of charters. The river will give you the best day of your life and then try to drown your cousin. You respect it or you don\'t come back.',
    'People ask about the plaque. Eleven names. I think about the twelfth. Boy in \'91. I had his wrist. Then I didn\'t. Every year I drop a line in that spot. No bait. I\'m not fishing for anything.'],
  bobby: ['Shrimp\'s live, crickets are loud, coffee\'s from this morning. Choose wisely.',
    'You came back! Most people come in once for the restroom and that\'s it. You\'re a customer now. Want a hat?',
    'Rose painted the old pump like a fish. Said if a gas station has to die it might as well die smiling. She was funnier than me. I just sell worms.',
    'Rose ran the shop her last summer from that lawn chair. Chemo Mondays, bait the rest of the week. Sold more sitting down than I ever did standing. People think the chair\'s for customers. It\'s not for customers.'],
  johnson: ['Wait for the sign, baby. I don\'t care if you\'re police. The sign is the sign.',
    'You stop for my kids every morning. I notice. Most cars I just stare at till they get religion.',
    'Took this job the year my boy started kindergarten across this street. Couldn\'t stand him crossing without me. He\'s thirty-four now. Firefighter. I\'m still here. Nobody told me when to stop.',
    'He honks when he passes on his way to the station. Two short, one long. That\'s been "love you, Mama" since he was sixteen. With jobs like his and yours, someday it\'ll be quiet. I don\'t let myself think about it much. But I count the honks.'],
  miguel: ['Doors are open, the coffee\'s weak, and nobody here will ask why you came. Welcome.',
    'You look tired. That\'s not a sermon, just an observation. Sit ten minutes. God won\'t mind. Neither will I.',
    'I was a public defender before the collar. Ninety cases at a time. Most people in front of a judge aren\'t evil. They\'re out of options. Now I try to be one more option.',
    'Something I don\'t say from the pulpit: I\'ve lost my faith twice. Once in a courtroom, once at a hospital bed. Both times it came back the same way. Somebody showed up with a casserole. That\'s the whole theology. Show up with the casserole.'],
  sam: ['Don\'t park in front of the bay doors. I know you\'re police. The truck doesn\'t.',
    'Cadet! We\'re grilling Thursday. Everybody says they\'ll come. Nobody comes. Prove me wrong.',
    'The firefighter who carried me out when I was eight, his name\'s Frank. Found him at a retirement party and said, "You don\'t know me, but." We do Thanksgiving every year now. He makes terrible stuffing. I eat all of it.',
    'Last year I carried a kid out. Same age I was. Same smoke in his hair. After, I sat in the truck and shook for an hour, and the guys left me alone, which is the kindest thing firefighters know how to do. Somewhere there\'s a kid who\'ll come find me someday. I hope I\'m around.'],
  gus: ['Shh. You\'ll spook Margaret. She\'s a redfish. She\'s been ignoring me since 2009.',
    'Caught nothing today. Nothing yesterday. Best two days of the week. You learn to like the waiting.',
    'My wife sat right there and read while I fished. Fifty-one years. Now I bring her book and read it out loud to the river. The fish don\'t mind. Some of them like the mysteries.',
    'Doctor says my heart\'s tired. I said so\'s the river and it keeps going. But when it\'s time, I want it here, a line in the water and the sun going down. Not a hospital. Somebody ought to know that, and it can\'t be my kids. They\'d argue.'],
  gerald: ['Hard hats past the yellow line. I don\'t make the rules. I enforce them. With a clipboard.',
    'Pallet count\'s off by one. It\'s always off by one on Tuesdays. I\'ll find it. I always find it.',
    'Everybody laughed at the clipboard. Then the theft ring, and the FBI wants to borrow it. I made them sign for it. On the clipboard.',
    'My dad couldn\'t read. Worked this port twenty years, memorized everything, hid it. They switched to written manifests in \'87 and that was that. I write everything down because he couldn\'t. Every page is a little bit for him.'],
  rusty: ['Got ID? Got a receipt? Then we can talk. No ID, no receipt, no copper. No hard feelings.',
    'Fella tried to sell me a church bell yesterday. A church bell. I called it in before he finished the sentence.',
    'They call my yard the snitch pile. I put it on a T-shirt. Sold forty. Mostly to cops. Want one? Medium?',
    'I bought stolen goods once. 1994. Tools off a widow\'s porch. Her husband\'s. She came looking, cried in my office, and I said nothing. Thirty years I\'ve turned down every dirty dollar, because I can\'t give hers back.'],
  dee: ['Lot\'s for rigs, honey. Park that cruiser where a trailer can\'t flatten it.',
    'You again. Go ahead, pull my logs. They\'re perfect. I\'ll wait.',
    'Two point four million miles. You see everybody\'s worst day out there. Wrecks, fires, a man in a wedding dress hitchhiking through Georgia. So you wave at everybody. You don\'t know what they\'re driving away from.',
    'Diesel was my husband\'s. Wendell died on a run outside Amarillo. Heart, right there in the cab. That dog sat with him six hours till the trooper came. Diesel rides with me now. He\'s why I\'m careful. That dog can\'t lose both of us.'],
  carl: ['Closed after nine. Unless you\'re police. You\'re police. Okay. Still closed, but politely.',
    'Forty-one thousand steps last night. Doctor says I\'m in great shape. Wife says I\'m never home. Both right.',
    'Three break-ins stopped and I never touched anybody. I was just standing somewhere they didn\'t expect. That\'s the whole trick.',
    'Fourteen years as a guard at county jail before this. Brought it home every night. Here it\'s boxes and locks. Sometimes I think I took this job so I could guard things that can\'t get hurt.'],
  shonda: ['Cans in blue, glass in green, and no pizza boxes. Grease is the enemy.',
    'You recycle at the station? You don\'t. Nobody does. One day I\'m showing up with bins. Fair warning.',
    'Mama started this with a pickup and the cans after church. People called her the garbage lady. That garbage paid for my college. I drive her truck on Sundays so it doesn\'t forget her.',
    'At the end she didn\'t know me most days. But she\'d see a can on the ground and pick it up and put it in her pocket. Every time. Some part of her still knew the job. I wonder what part of me will still be working when the rest forgets.'],
  lupe: ['Menu hasn\'t changed since \'74. No substitutions. Mama is listening from somewhere.',
    'Number four, extra rice, no onion. See? I remember. I remember everybody\'s no onion.',
    'Grandmother, mother, me. Same pots. I scratched my initials next to theirs on the big one. Someday somebody else will. I hope they don\'t change the menu.',
    'I don\'t have kids. People ask who gets the Pail after me. Some nights I lock up and stand in the kitchen and hear all three of us. Then I think about it being quiet forever and I have to sit down. Anyway. Dessert? It\'s flan. It\'s always flan.'],
  moore: ['Phones off. Hats off. Gum out. Thank you.',
    'You stood when the judge came in. Most visitors don\'t. I noticed.',
    'Nineteen years, one word. "No." Said it once to a man halfway out the side door and he sat back down. I\'ve lived off that ever since. Personally, I cry at commercials.',
    'Hardest part is the families after sentencing. Somebody\'s mother, somebody\'s daughter. I\'m supposed to keep order. Mostly that means handing out tissues and pretending I didn\'t. The county doesn\'t buy them. I do.'],
  barnes: ['If you\'re serving papers, wrong office. If you\'re asking questions, also wrong office.',
    'Clean report last month. Ruiz showed me. I was very annoyed. Nothing to cross-examine.',
    'We\'re not on opposite sides, you and me. Same old argument: is the government allowed to do this to a person? Your answer should be "maybe." Mine is "prove it."',
    'I lost a trial in 2003 I should have won. He did eleven years. DNA cleared him. He sends me a Christmas card every year thanking me. Thanking me. I can\'t open them anymore. Ruiz opens them and reads them to me.'],
  ray: ['Need a bond, a lawyer, or directions? I\'m good at two of those.',
    'Client of mine says you treated him decent on a stop. Said it twice. He doesn\'t say anything twice.',
    'I bailed out my own brother in \'98. Everybody said don\'t. He\'s a pastor in Mobile now. Second chances aren\'t a policy. They\'re a bet. You lose some. You keep betting.',
    'I\'ve lost clients. Overdose, mostly. I go to every funeral and sit in the back. Families look at me like, who\'s the bail guy? But I was the last one who believed in some of them. Somebody who did should be there.'],
  hattie: ['Take a number. Yes, you too. The number machine has no rank.',
    'You spelled your own name wrong on a form last week. I fixed it. You\'re welcome. Don\'t do it again.',
    'First filing I ever stamped, 1984, a parking ticket appeal. The man won. I kept it. Every piece of paper in this building is somebody\'s whole day.',
    'One afternoon I stamped my own divorce. Same stamp, same desk. Then the next person in line had an adoption. I stamped that too. Life doesn\'t wait for your feelings, sugar. You just make sure the date\'s right.'],
  ramos: ['Sally port\'s restricted. Badge, then wait on the line. The line means the line.',
    'You searched the back seat before you loaded him? Good. You\'d be surprised who doesn\'t.',
    'Pen cap, 2001. Man slipped his cuffs with a pen cap somebody left back there. I was the somebody. Nobody got hurt. I got lucky. Lucky isn\'t a plan.',
    'Transport\'s the longest twenty minutes of somebody\'s life. One man asked if I\'d drive past his mother\'s house so he could see her porch light. I couldn\'t detour, policy\'s policy. But there were two routes the same length, and I took the one on her street. He saw it. Neither of us said anything.'],
  exhibita: ['(The cat assesses you and finds you beneath notice.)',
    '(The cat permits you to exist within three feet of her. This is a significant development.)',
    '(The cat brings you one rubber band from the clerk\'s office and sets it on your boot. You have been entered into evidence.)',
    '(The cat climbs into your lap and falls asleep. Clerk Hattie, passing, whispers, "She\'s never done that. Not even for me." She does not sound happy about it.)'],
  walt: ['Route 9, all stops. Exact fare or a really good story. I\'ve heard most of the stories.',
    'Ride along sometime. I\'ll show you where everybody in this city really goes. Spoiler: the pharmacy.',
    'Seven trips in the flood. People say brave. It wasn\'t brave. I knew the route better than the water did. Mrs. Ochoa called me mijo the whole way. I\'m sixty-three.',
    'Trip seven, the water came over the steps. One gentleman wouldn\'t leave without his wife\'s ashes. I went back in for them, waist deep. Not telling you to be brave. Telling you some things matter more to people than you\'d guess, and the job is taking them seriously.'],
  tasha: ['I\'m retired. Don\'t tell me your symptoms. ...Fine. Which arm?',
    'You\'re not drinking enough water. I can tell by your face. Thirty years of faces.',
    'The depot fire, nineteen patients in two hours. One was a young officer named Okonkwo who kept apologizing for bleeding on my shoes. He sends flowers every year. Still apologizes in the card.',
    'The one I think about isn\'t from the fire. A Tuesday. Old man, his heart, four in the morning, and nobody came for him. Nobody. I sat with him till they took him, because nobody should go alone. If you ever get the chance, sit with them.'],
  leon: ['Floor\'s wet. My fault. Walk on the dry side, I left you a path.',
    'The kids drew you. Well, a police officer. Very long arms. I don\'t think they\'ve seen one up close.',
    'Two thousand drawings in nineteen years. My wife told me to throw some out. I said which ones? She looked for an hour and couldn\'t pick. Now she wants more shelves.',
    'I was a kid nobody saw. Back row, quiet. The custodian at my school, Mr. Abernathy, said "Good morning, Leon" every day. That was all. Some days it was the only time anybody said my name. So I learn all of theirs. Every kid.'],
  carmen: ['You hungry? Don\'t lie. Nobody climbs four floors and isn\'t hungry.',
    'Sit, sit. Eat first, then tell me what\'s wrong. You always come with a face like something\'s wrong.',
    'I came after María with one suitcase. Clothes, a photo, my mother\'s caldero. People say, only three things, Carmen? The pot was the important one. The pot makes the family. Look how many I have now.',
    'My house in Utuado is gone. The whole hill. I have no pictures of the kitchen, and that\'s what goes first in my memory. So I cook it. Every day, how it smelled in there. If I stop, I lose it. You understand? I\'m not feeding them. I\'m remembering.'],
  priya: ['Spice levels one to five. Five is not a dare. Please don\'t make five a dare.',
    'Level three again? Brave. Not level-five brave. Smart brave.',
    'Eleven years an engineer. Bridges. I ate lunch alone at my desk every day thinking, I connect people for a living and I talk to nobody. Now I talk to three hundred people a day. Best bridge I ever built is this window.',
    'My father said I was wasting my degree. A year, he didn\'t speak to me. Then one day he\'s in my line like everybody else. Orders a five. Eats the whole thing crying, from the spice, he says. Pays full price. He has never said he\'s proud. He comes every Thursday.'],
  hollis: ['You play? No? Sit anyway. Watching is how everybody starts.',
    'Your knight\'s all over the board, cadet. Like you on a call. Think before you move.',
    'Nine years inside, chess was the only place things made sense. Nobody lies about what a bishop can do. Out here is harder. You never know what anybody\'s allowed to do.',
    'I tell every kid "think before you move" because I didn\'t. Nineteen years old, one night, one move. I\'ve had a long time to think about it since. That\'s the move you don\'t get back. Somebody should have been sitting here when I was nineteen. So I sit here.'],
  mo: ['Pump four\'s broken, pump two is slow, and coffee\'s free for police, because my cameras love you.',
    'I saved the last good sandwich for you. Egg salad. I know. Nobody wants egg salad. I saved it anyway.',
    'The thank-you letters from the department, I keep in the back where customers can\'t see. If they see them, they think I\'m a snitch. If I don\'t keep them, I forget why I do it.',
    'My father had a station like this back home. Lost it. Came here and pumped gas for another man twenty years so I could own one. He died the month I signed the papers. Every morning I unlock the door and say, Baba, we\'re open.'],
  bea: ['Glazed, chocolate, or the sprinkles that grown men pretend are for their kids?',
    'Five a.m. again. You know what happens to people who eat donuts at five a.m.? They become regulars. Too late.',
    'My mother\'s recipe, from her bakery in Mexico City. Every December I make one batch her way, by hand, all night, and give it all away. My knees hate me. Mama would have done two.',
    'Day-olds go to the shelter at nine. Twenty years. One morning a man in a suit buys a dozen fresh and tells me he ate my day-olds at the shelter in 2006. Says it was the only thing that tasted like somebody cared. I had to go in the back. The fryer\'s good cover.'],
  lewis: ['Retired. Don\'t hand me anything to deliver. My legs will try out of habit.',
    'Mail piling up on a porch. That\'s first. Then the curtains. Then the smell. You learn the order.',
    'Thirty-three years on foot. You know what a mail carrier really is? The only person who visits every house every day. For some folks I was the only person, period.',
    'Mrs. Gaines on Birch wrote herself letters. Took me a year to notice. Same hand, mailed from her own corner. She just wanted something with her name on it. So I wrote to her. "A friend from the route." Eleven years. Her daughter found them after. She came to find me. I still don\'t know what to say about that.'],
  andy: ['Pull up a chair. Board\'s free, coffee isn\'t, lessons are free if you don\'t argue.',
    'You open with the same move every time. I respect consistency. I also beat consistency.',
    'That photo by the trophies is my unit. Gulf, \'91. Four of us played every night in the tent. I\'m the only one still playing. Some nights I play their openings. Keeps them in the game.',
    'Came home and couldn\'t sit in a room without facing the door. Couldn\'t sleep. Chess was the only thing that slowed my head down to one problem at a time. That\'s what I teach these kids, really. Not chess. One problem at a time.'],
  sunny: ['Oranges are a dollar. Honor box is right there. Yes, it works. People are mostly good.',
    'You came back! Take a bag. You look like someone who doesn\'t eat fruit.',
    'Eleven developers. The last one says, "Everybody has a number." I said my grandfather\'s number was "the oranges outlive us." He didn\'t know what to do with that.',
    'Honest truth? I\'m tired. The farm barely breaks even. Some nights I take the last offer out of the drawer and read it. Then I walk out to the grove and the blossoms are going and it smells like being six. Then I put it back. Some years that\'s the whole business plan.'],
  jamal: ['Requests go in the jar. Bribes also go in the jar. Same jar.',
    'Officer on the dance floor! Kidding. Unless? Saturday. I\'ll play something you can walk to.',
    'Six years, no fights. People think that\'s luck. It\'s the playlist. Crowd gets hot, you slide in something their grandma loves. Hard to throw a punch while your auntie\'s singing.',
    'My cousin got shot in a parking lot after a party when I was fifteen. I was the DJ. Fifteen. Ever since, I read a room like my life depends on it. Every Saturday is me trying to give that night a better ending.'],
  ines: ['If it bites, tell me before I\'m holding it, please.',
    'How\'s your K-9 unit doing? Tell him there\'s a peanut butter thing in my fridge with his name on it.',
    'Retiring police dogs get a cake, peanut butter and pumpkin. The handlers cry every time. Big tough people. I keep tissues next to the cake now.',
    'The hardest one is the last appointment. Handler on the floor, dog\'s head in his lap. One sergeant stayed four hours and told that dog every call they ever worked together. I stayed too. Clinic was closed. Didn\'t matter.'],
  pearl: ['Speak up, I\'m ninety-one. No, don\'t, I can hear fine. I just like to see who\'ll do it.',
    'You\'re the one who stops to talk. People think old means boring. Old means I have the gossip from 1952.',
    'My house is under that river. They moved the channel in \'49. At low tide you can see the chimney. Everyone says it\'s a piling. Everyone is wrong.',
    'I\'ve outlived two husbands, a son, and every friend I made before 1980. People ask the secret. There isn\'t one. You keep showing up for whoever\'s left. Right now that\'s Eli and a cadet. Lucky me. I mean that.'],
  nash: ['Stay off the infield. It doesn\'t care that you\'re police. Neither do I, much.',
    'You stand like a catcher. Knees bent. ...Oh. That\'s just your duty belt.',
    'Seven years in the minors, never got the call. Now I mow this diamond and stripe it every spring like the big leagues do. Closest I\'ll get. Close enough, most days.',
    'The day I quit, my dad drove four hours to get me. Not a word the whole way. Then at a gas station he buys me a Coke and says, "You were good. That\'s not nothing." He\'s been gone ten years. When I finish the stripes I say it out loud. That\'s not nothing.'],
  cora: ['Vanilla, chocolate, or blue. Blue is a flavor. Ask any kid.',
    'You\'ve got a lost-kid face. That\'s a compliment. Lost kids walk up to faces like yours.',
    'Forty-one stars inside the lid. Each one a kid I got back to their folks. Number one was 1998. She teaches second grade now. Brings her class every May.',
    'I was lost once. County fair, 1971. Six years old, waited by the Ferris wheel half the night. A lady with an ice cream cart found me. That\'s why the cart. I\'m not really selling ice cream. I\'m standing where a lost kid would look.'],
  eli: ['Life jacket on or no canoe. You can look silly or you can look drowned.',
    'River\'s high today. See the line on the piling? Green means no. It\'s green.',
    'Twenty years, never lost a customer. People think I\'m uptight about the jackets. I am. Extremely. Proudly.',
    'Kenny Ortiz. We were sixteen. He didn\'t wear one because I didn\'t wear one, because nobody cool did. I made it to the bank. Now I tell every customer: if you won\'t wear it for you, wear it for whoever would have to tell your mom.'],
  toby: ['Nothing for you today, officer. Unless you\'re Mrs. Albright. Are you Mrs. Albright?',
    'You look at porches now, don\'t you? Mail, curtains, papers. Mr. Lewis got to you too.',
    'First week, Mr. Lewis made me learn every dog\'s name on the route. I said that\'s not the job. He said that is exactly the job. The dogs vouch for you.',
    'Mr. Baptiste on Willow was on the floor two days before I noticed the mail. He made it. Told me after he\'d been yelling and nobody heard. Now I knock every day. Regulations don\'t say I have to. He makes me coffee. It\'s terrible. I drink all of it.'],
  tess: ['Yard\'s live. Don\'t cross unless you want to be the next training video.',
    'Hear that? Coupling on track four. You\'ll hear it in your sleep tonight. Everybody does.',
    'My grandmother threw these switches in \'43. Her initials are carved in the window frame. E.R. I touch them at the start of every shift. Feels like checking in.',
    'She said in the war she\'d watch the troop trains and say a prayer for every car. Didn\'t know a soul on them. Thought somebody should. I do it for the freight now. Lumber, grain, cars. It feels dumb. But every car is headed to somebody.'],
  hector: ['Sparks fly far. Behind the line or lose your eyebrows. Your call.',
    'Want to try? Mask down. Don\'t look at the light. Everybody looks at the light.',
    'A hundred and some kids through this garage. One welded Station 3\'s flagpole. I take the long way to work so I pass it. Don\'t tell Lupe. She thinks the long way is for her.',
    'My own boy went the other way. Couldn\'t reach him. Prison, then out, then... I don\'t know where. So every kid who walks in, I teach like he\'s my son. I wasn\'t a good father to one. I\'m trying to be one to a hundred. It doesn\'t add up. I know.'],
  ivy: ['Slow movements by the hives. Bees don\'t hate you. They\'re just very busy.',
    'Here. Spoon. That\'s clover. Next week will taste like orange blossom. Same bees, different week.',
    'Started for my son\'s allergies. Local honey. He outgrew them at twelve. I didn\'t outgrow the bees. Some things you start for someone and keep for yourself.',
    'He lives far away now. Calls when he remembers, which isn\'t often. The bees though. Every evening, forty thousand of them coming home. They always come home. I know it\'s not the same. Some evenings it\'s enough.'],
  wanda: ['Shh. Painted bunting. Don\'t move. ...And it\'s gone. Thanks.',
    'You\'re on my list now. Officer, common. Easily startled. Seen in pairs.',
    'Everyone says I was brave to report that man burying the safe in \'97. Mostly I was annoyed. He scared off a woodpecker.',
    'I started birding after my husband left. Needed a reason to get up that wasn\'t waiting by the phone. Sit still long enough and things come to you. Birds, mostly. Some people. Hector. You.'],
  pat: ['Freight dispatch. If you\'re not a truck, I can\'t help you. Are you a truck?',
    'Your channel bleeds through sometimes. Your dispatcher\'s good. Velvet. I\'d know that voice anywhere.',
    'Velvet and I have never met. Thirty years. We talk every Christmas Eve. Worst calls of the year first, then the funniest. Always end on the funniest.',
    'I asked her once if we should meet. She said, "Pat, if we meet we\'re two old ladies. On the phone we\'re legends." So we don\'t. That\'s the radio for you. Everybody\'s closer than they are.'],
  lou: ['Bay\'s hot. Don\'t touch anything orange.',
    'Had a truck in today, the brake pads were a memory. The jar of bad bolts is almost full.',
    'I don\'t care about trucks. I care about somebody\'s kid not getting run over by a man who couldn\'t afford brakes.',
    'My brother drove. Bad tire. Company skipped the inspection. I was twenty. Got certified the next year. Every truck out of this bay, I sign my own name. Not the shop\'s. Mine. If something happens, it comes to me.'],
  alvarez: ['Retired. Please don\'t call me Your Honor. Do call me if you see my reading glasses.',
    'First time you testify, your hands shake. Fifth time, your voice. Twentieth time nothing shakes, and that\'s when you have to be careful.',
    'I threw out Castillo\'s case in \'95. Report didn\'t match the testimony. He was furious. Ten years later he sent a note: "Best lesson I ever got." It\'s still in my robe pocket. The robe\'s in a closet.',
    'In 1990 I gave a nineteen-year-old twenty years because the law said so, and I didn\'t fight it. He got out last spring. I went to the gate and said, "I\'m sorry." He said, "For what?" I couldn\'t answer him. I\'m still working on the answer.'],
  nico: ['Objection Roast, Sustained Blend, or the decaf we call Mistrial?',
    'Double shot. You look like you testified at nine. Did you testify at nine? I can always tell.',
    'First in my class, night school. I study between the espresso rushes. The judges tip in legal advice. Mostly about parking.',
    'Dad notarized papers in this building for thirty years for people who couldn\'t afford a lawyer. When I pass the bar I want him to stamp my oath. They say that\'s not how it works. I\'m asking anyway.'],
  greer: ['Speak clearly and don\'t talk over each other. That\'s all I ask of anyone, in court or out.',
    'You say "um" four times a minute on the stand. I counted. I count everyone. Sorry. Habit.',
    'Eleven hundred trials. I remember the words, not the faces. I\'d know a defendant\'s sentence anywhere and walk right past him at the grocery store.',
    'Some testimony I typed with my eyes closed. My fingers kept going. That\'s the job: the record doesn\'t stop because it hurts. When I get home my husband reads me the comics out loud. Every night. Twenty-eight years.'],
  felix: ['Two forms of ID, sign in front of me, not before. It\'s the law. It\'s also my personality.',
    'You\'d be amazed what people want notarized. Yesterday, an adoption agreement for a goldfish. It was valid.',
    'I caught that forged deed because the "signer" died the year before. I knew. I notarized her will. Big city, but you remember the people who sit in your chair.',
    'One of the widows got her house back and asked me to notarize a thank-you card. Not a legal document. A card. I stamped it anyway. Best thing I ever put my seal on.'],
  ada: ['Records Annex. If it happened in this county, it\'s in a box. Possibly the wrong box.',
    'You search like a detective. Bottom shelves first. Nobody checks the bottom shelves.',
    'I found the founding charter looking for a parking ticket. That\'s archives. You never find what you\'re looking for. You find what was waiting for you.',
    'I keep a box for people nobody came for. Unclaimed estates. Photos, letters. I index them anyway: name, dates, one line about who they were if I can tell. Somebody should be able to find them. Even if nobody does.'],
  booker: ['Hat off near the wall. Whoever you are. Hat off.',
    'You stopped and read a name last time. Most people walk past. I notice who reads.',
    'Three names up there I knew. I polish theirs last. Slowest. Davis would laugh and say I\'m wasting rag.',
    'I was supposed to be on that patrol. Swapped with Davis because of a toothache. A toothache. Fifty-some years and it\'s still the dumbest, biggest thing that ever happened to me. So I keep the wall clean. Only shift I can still cover for him.']
};
export const HEART_TIER = (f) => (f >= 6 ? 3 : f >= 3 ? 2 : f >= 1 ? 1 : 0);

